(customer daily running balance → 30‑day rolling avg → monthly max per customer → summed per month)

• customer_transactions.txn_date : transaction calendar date for sequencing and daily buckets  
• customer_transactions.customer_id : customer identifier for partitioning calculations  
• customer_transactions.txn_type : distinguishes “deposit” ( + ) from all other types ( – )  
• customer_transactions.txn_amount : dollar value applied (with sign logic driven by txn_type)  

Typical conditions you will probably use later  
• WHERE txn_type = 'deposit'       --  add to balance  
• WHERE txn_type <> 'deposit'      --  subtract from balance  
• WHERE txn_date >= '2020-01-01'   --  limit to study period if needed  
• WHERE customer_id = ?            --  per–customer windows  
• HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT txn_date) ≥ 30  -- ensure at least 30 days of history before averaging  

Below are ten exploratory SQLite queries (simple → more complex) to familiarise yourself with the data that will feed the final calculation.  
Each query is limited to 20 rows and uses DISTINCT wherever practical.

```sql
-- 1. Description: List all distinct transaction types that exist.
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_type"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Show 20 unique customer IDs to gauge table size.
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Check distinct years present in the data (substring first 4 chars of txn_date).
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR("txn_date", 1, 4) AS "txn_year"
FROM "customer_transactions"
ORDER BY "txn_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Inspect 20 distinct sample dates for one random customer (e.g., id = 1).
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_date"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "customer_id" = 1
ORDER BY "txn_date"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Retrieve 20 distinct month–year buckets in YYYY‑MM format to see monthly spread.
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR("txn_date", 1, 7) AS "year_month"
FROM "customer_transactions"
ORDER BY "year_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Show the first transaction date for 20 customers (use MIN aggregate in a subquery).
SELECT DISTINCT c."customer_id", c."first_txn_date"
FROM (
     SELECT "customer_id", MIN("txn_date") AS "first_txn_date"
     FROM "customer_transactions"
     GROUP BY "customer_id"
     LIMIT 20
) AS c;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: List distinct combinations of customer, date and transaction type to confirm uniqueness.
SELECT DISTINCT "customer_id", "txn_date", "txn_type"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Grab distinct non‑deposit transaction types (anything other than 'deposit').
SELECT DISTINCT "txn_type"
FROM "customer_transactions"
WHERE "txn_type" <> 'deposit'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Preview 20 distinct (customer, amount sign) pairs to verify sign later.
SELECT DISTINCT
       "customer_id",
       CASE WHEN "txn_type" = 'deposit' THEN '+' ELSE '-' END AS "sign"
FROM "customer_transactions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: For one customer, list distinct day totals (sum amount per day) to eyeball balance building blocks.
SELECT DISTINCT
       daily_totals."customer_id",
       daily_totals."txn_date",
       daily_totals."day_sum"
FROM (
     SELECT "customer_id",
            "txn_date",
            SUM(CASE WHEN "txn_type" = 'deposit'
                     THEN  "txn_amount"
                     ELSE - "txn_amount"
                END) AS "day_sum"
     FROM "customer_transactions"
     WHERE "customer_id" = 1
     GROUP BY "customer_id", "txn_date"
     LIMIT 20
) AS daily_totals
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries should give you a thorough feel for the dataset before writing the window‑function logic required for the final rolling‑average calculation.
